 "notes": "Second read 2026-10-26. The old parse labelled every list 'JV', swapped genders on two lists and dropped the tied rows. Rewritten from the sheet's ranking tables (cell by cell): per gender, the ranked fastest varsity relay legs (girls 16 rows, boys 21 rows, tied places kept: girls 10 Lianna Rubinoff/Kelsey Rex, 15 Dellatore/Chamberlain; boys 3 Martin/Tinkham, 10 Robinson/Quentin, 12 Oringo/Knowland, 17 Britten/Graves, 20 Belanger/Woods), then the JV list and the Freshmen list (10 each, surnames only as printed; the two boys JV rows whose name is printed 'Unknown' (Cheverus, 9:13 place 6 and 9:22 place 10) are left out as unnamed). Times were printed by Excel as h:mm:ss with ':00' appended ('9:23:00' is 9:23); written m:ss. The relay leg length is not printed (later SMAA Relays are 1.55-1.6 miles), so events keep no distance. Not entered: the 7-leg (boys) and 5-leg (girls) grids of each team's individual legs (no place; relay leg sheets) and the team total-time columns (relay totals in h:mm:ss, not points). Gender per block: the girls block is the first table (Bonny Eagle's Lauren Dudley first), boys the second (Jesse Young first). No date printed; left null (sub5 index has Aug 30/31 2007). THIRD READ (leftover check): every ranked row re-checked against the sheet's ranking tables (girls 16, JV 10, freshman 10; boys 21, JV 8, freshman 10; all names, schools and mm:ss times agree). The 35 'implausible' flags came from these fastest-leg marks being read as 5K times; they are relay legs of the SMAA Relays, which the same series ran at 1.55 miles in 2005, 2006 and 2009 (events named '1.55 Mile Run CC' there; 2007 fastest legs boys 7:48 / girls 9:23 sit with 2005 8:03 / 9:14, 2006 8:00 / 8:47, 2009 7:43 / 8:53), so the six events are now named 1.55 Mile Run CC (with JV / Freshman). The sheet itself prints no distance. 2026-10-28 orchestrator: leg distance NOT set: the SMAA relay leg changed most years (2009 1.55, 2012 1.6, 2013 1.65, 2014-16 1.54, 2017 1.5, 2019 1.77 Mile), so another year's length is no evidence for 2007; the events stay without a distance."
